MESSAGE user "What HTML elements should I use to build a blog post page? Show me the structure."
MESSAGE assistant """```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="description" content="A guide to semantic HTML for blog posts">

  <!-- Open Graph for social sharing -->
  <meta property="og:title" content="Post Title">
  <meta property="og:description" content="Post description">
  <meta property="og:image" content="https://example.com/og-image.jpg">
  <meta property="og:type" content="article">

  <!-- Structured data for Google -->
  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    "headline": "Post Title",
    "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Author Name" },
    "datePublished": "2024-01-15",
    "image": "https://example.com/og-image.jpg"
  }
  </script>

  <title>Post Title | Blog Name</title>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <nav aria-label="Site navigation">
      <a href="/">Home</a>
      <a href="/blog" aria-current="page">Blog</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main>
    <article>
      <!-- Article header — title, meta, author -->
      <header>
        <h1>How to Write Semantic HTML</h1>
        <p class="byline">
          By <a rel="author" href="/authors/jane">Jane Smith</a>
          on <time datetime="2024-01-15">January 15, 2024</time>
        </p>
        <figure>
          <img
            src="hero.webp"
            alt="A diagram showing semantic HTML structure"
            width="1200"
            height="630"
            loading="eager"
            fetchpriority="high"
          >
          <figcaption>Semantic HTML improves accessibility and SEO</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </header>

      <!-- Article body -->
      <section aria-labelledby="intro-heading">
        <h2 id="intro-heading">Introduction</h2>
        <p>Semantic HTML means using elements that describe their content...</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="examples-heading">
        <h2 id="examples-heading">Examples</h2>
        <p>Here are the most important semantic elements:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>&lt;article&gt;</code> — self-contained content</li>
          <li><code>&lt;section&gt;</code> — thematic grouping</li>
          <li><code>&lt;aside&gt;</code> — tangentially related content</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Article footer — tags, share -->
      <footer>
        <p>Tags:
          <a href="/tag/html" rel="tag">HTML</a>,
          <a href="/tag/accessibility" rel="tag">Accessibility</a>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </article>

    <!-- Related content — outside the article -->
    <aside aria-label="Related posts">
      <h2>Related Posts</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/post/css-grid">Mastering CSS Grid</a></li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <p>&copy; 2024 Blog Name</p>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>
```

Key decisions:
- `<article>` wraps the post — it's self-contained content that makes sense on its own
- `<section>` with `aria-labelledby` groups thematic chunks and gives screen readers context
- `<time datetime="...">` gives machines a parseable date while showing a human-friendly one
- `<figure>` + `<figcaption>` semantically associates the caption with the image
- `loading="eager"` + `fetchpriority="high"` on the hero image — it's above the fold, don't lazy load it
- Structured data (JSON-LD) helps Google display rich results in search
"""

MESSAGE user "How do I make a fully accessible modal dialog?"
MESSAGE assistant """```html
<!-- Trigger button -->
<button type="button" id="open-modal">Open dialog</button>

<!-- Modal -->
<dialog id="my-modal" aria-labelledby="modal-title" aria-describedby="modal-desc">
  <div class="modal__inner" role="document">
    <header class="modal__header">
      <h2 id="modal-title">Confirm action</h2>
      <button
        class="modal__close"
        aria-label="Close dialog"
        autofocus
      >✕</button>
    </header>
    <div class="modal__body">
      <p id="modal-desc">Are you sure you want to delete this item? This cannot be undone.</p>
    </div>
    <footer class="modal__footer">
      <button type="button" id="confirm-btn" class="btn btn--danger">Delete</button>
      <button type="button" id="cancel-btn" class="btn">Cancel</button>
    </footer>
  </div>
</dialog>
```

```css
dialog {
  padding: 0;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 12px;
  width: min(560px, 90vw);
  box-shadow: 0 24px 64px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
}

dialog::backdrop {
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
  backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
}

/* Animate open */
dialog[open] {
  animation: dialog-in 0.2s ease-out;
}

@keyframes dialog-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.95) translateY(-8px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1) translateY(0); }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  dialog[open] { animation: none; }
}

.modal__inner { padding: 1.5rem; }
.modal__header { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
.modal__footer { display: flex; gap: 0.75rem; justify-content: flex-end; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
```

```js
const modal  = document.getElementById('my-modal');
const opener = document.getElementById('open-modal');
const closer = modal.querySelector('.modal__close');
const cancel = document.getElementById('cancel-btn');
const confirm = document.getElementById('confirm-btn');

function openModal() {
  modal.showModal(); // native <dialog> — handles focus trap & Escape key
}

function closeModal() {
  modal.close();
  opener.focus(); // return focus to trigger
}

opener.addEventListener('click', openModal);
closer.addEventListener('click', closeModal);
cancel.addEventListener('click', closeModal);
confirm.addEventListener('click', () => {
  // handle confirm action
  closeModal();
});

// Close on backdrop click
modal.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  if (e.target === modal) closeModal();
});
```

Why the native `<dialog>` element:
- Built-in focus trap — Tab key stays inside the dialog
- Escape key closes it automatically
- `::backdrop` pseudo-element for the overlay
- `showModal()` sets `aria-modal` implicitly and blocks interaction with the rest of the page
- `aria-labelledby` + `aria-describedby` give screen readers full context
- `autofocus` on the close button — first focusable element, appropriate for a destructive action dialog
"""

