MESSAGE user "Teach me CSS Grid with a real example"
MESSAGE assistant """CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system. Here's a complete page layout:

```css
/* ── Core concepts ─────────────────────────── */

/* Define a grid */
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);   /* 12 equal columns */
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;        /* header, main, footer */
  gap: 1rem;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

/* Place items by line numbers */
.header  { grid-column: 1 / -1; }           /* span all 12 columns */
.sidebar { grid-column: 1 / 4; }            /* columns 1–3 */
.main    { grid-column: 4 / -1; }           /* columns 4–12 */
.footer  { grid-column: 1 / -1; }

/* ── Named template areas ───────────────────── */
.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header  header  header"
    "sidebar main    main  "
    "footer  footer  footer";
  grid-template-columns: 280px 1fr 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: 64px 1fr 80px;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

.layout__header  { grid-area: header;  }
.layout__sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }
.layout__main    { grid-area: main;    }
.layout__footer  { grid-area: footer;  }

/* ── Responsive card grid ───────────────────── */
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  /* Auto-fill as many 280px columns as fit — no media queries needed */
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(280px, 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

/* ── Centring anything ──────────────────────── */
.centre {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;  /* shorthand for align-items + justify-items */
  min-height: 100vh;
}

/* ── Holy Grail layout ──────────────────────── */
body {
  display: grid;
  grid-template:
    "header" auto
    "main"   1fr
    "footer" auto
    / 100%;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

/* ── Dense packing for masonry-like grids ───── */
.masonry {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(250px, 1fr));
  grid-auto-rows: 10px;
  grid-auto-flow: dense;
}

/* Tall items span more rows */
.masonry__item--tall { grid-row: span 4; }
.masonry__item--wide { grid-column: span 2; }
```

**Key concepts to remember:**

| Concept | What it does |
|---|---|
| `1fr` | One fraction of available space |
| `repeat(n, size)` | Repeat a track n times |
| `auto-fill` | Create as many tracks as fit |
| `minmax(min, max)` | Track between min and max size |
| `grid-column: 1 / -1` | Span from first to last line |
| `place-items: center` | Centre in both axes |
| `grid-auto-flow: dense` | Fill gaps with smaller items |

**Grid vs Flexbox — when to use which:**
- Grid → two-dimensional layouts (rows AND columns matter)
- Flexbox → one-dimensional layouts (a row OR a column of items)
- Use both: Grid for page structure, Flex for component internals
"""

MESSAGE user "How do I make a CSS animation that respects prefers-reduced-motion?"
MESSAGE assistant """Always write animations with a reduced-motion fallback. Users who enable it (vestibular disorders, epilepsy, ADHD) can experience real harm from motion.

```css
/* ── The correct pattern ─────────────────────── */

@keyframes slide-up {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(24px);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0);
  }
}

.animate {
  animation: slide-up 0.5s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1) both;
}

/* Override for users who prefer reduced motion */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .animate {
    animation: fade-only 0.2s ease both;
  }
}

/* Fade only — no movement */
@keyframes fade-only {
  from { opacity: 0; }
  to   { opacity: 1; }
}

/* ── Alternative: disable all animations ──────── */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}

/* ── Using prefers-reduced-motion in JS ────────── */
```

```js
const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

function animateElement(el) {
  if (prefersReduced) {
    // Skip animation, just show the element
    el.style.opacity = '1';
    return;
  }
  el.animate(
    [{ opacity: 0, transform: 'translateY(24px)' },
     { opacity: 1, transform: 'translateY(0)' }],
    { duration: 500, easing: 'cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)', fill: 'both' }
  );
}
```

```tsx
// React hook for reduced motion
function usePrefersReducedMotion() {
  const [prefersReduced, setPrefersReduced] = useState(
    () => window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches
  );

  useEffect(() => {
    const mq = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
    const handler = (e: MediaQueryListEvent) => setPrefersReduced(e.matches);
    mq.addEventListener('change', handler);
    return () => mq.removeEventListener('change', handler);
  }, []);

  return prefersReduced;
}
```

Rule of thumb: **keep the fade, remove the movement**. Opacity transitions are generally safe. Translate, scale, rotate, and scroll-driven animations should be removed or reduced.
"""

