MESSAGE user "Debug this React component — it crashes on load:\n```jsx\nexport default function UserDashboard({ userId }) {\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\n  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)\n      .then(res => res.json())\n      .then(data => setUser(data))\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (user) {\n      setLoading(true)\n      fetch(`/api/posts?author=${user.id}`)\n        .then(res => res.json())\n        .then(data => {\n          setPosts(data)\n          setLoading(false)\n        })\n    }\n  }, [user]);\n\n  function handleDeletePost(id) {\n    setPosts(posts.filter(post => post.id === id));\n    fetch(`/api/posts/${id}`, { method: 'DELETE' });\n  }\n\n  const totalLikes = posts.map(post => post.likes).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Welcome {user.name}</h1>\n      <p>Total likes: {totalLikes}</p>\n      {loading && <p>Loading posts...</p>}\n      <ul>\n        {posts.map(post =>\n          <li>\n            <span>{post.title}</span>\n            <button onclick={() => handleDeletePost(post.id)}>Delete</button>\n          </li>\n        )}\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```"
MESSAGE assistant """There are **6 bugs**. Here is every single one:

---

**Bug 1 — CRASH: accessing `.name` on null (renders before fetch completes)**
```jsx
// ❌ user is null on first render — TypeError: Cannot read properties of null
<h1>Welcome {user.name}</h1>
```
```jsx
// ✅ Early return guard — guarantees user is non-null below this line
if (!user) return <p>Loading user...</p>;
```
Root cause: `useState(null)` initialises user as null. The component renders before the fetch resolves. Every async data value starts null — always guard it.

---

**Bug 2 — WRONG LOGIC: filter keeps the deleted post, removes all others**
```js
// ❌ === keeps matching id, removes everything else
setPosts(posts.filter(post => post.id === id));
```
```js
// ✅ !== removes only the post with matching id
setPosts(posts.filter(post => post.id !== id));
```

---

**Bug 3 — STALE CLOSURE: userId missing from useEffect dependency array**
```js
// ❌ if userId prop changes, effect never re-runs — shows stale user data
useEffect(() => {
  fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)...
}, []); // userId used but not listed
```
```js
// ✅ list every external value used inside the effect
}, [userId]);
```

---

**Bug 4 — NO ERROR HANDLING: loading state never resets on fetch failure**
```js
// ❌ if fetch throws, setLoading(false) never runs — spinner hangs forever
fetch(`/api/posts?author=${user.id}`)
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => { setPosts(data); setLoading(false); })
// no .catch()
```
```js
// ✅
.then(data => { setPosts(data); setLoading(false); })
.catch(() => setLoading(false));
```

---

**Bug 5 — TYPO: onclick is an HTML attribute, not a React event handler**
```jsx
// ❌ React uses camelCase synthetic events
<button onclick={() => handleDeletePost(post.id)}>Delete</button>
```
```jsx
// ✅
<button onClick={() => handleDeletePost(post.id)}>Delete</button>
```

---

**Bug 6 — MISSING KEY PROP: list items have no key**
```jsx
// ❌ React warns and reconciliation is incorrect
{posts.map(post => <li>...</li>)}
```
```jsx
// ✅ use the unique stable id
{posts.map(post => <li key={post.id}>...</li>)}
```

---

**Fully corrected component:**
```tsx
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

type User  = { id: string; name: string };
type Post  = { id: string; title: string; likes: number };

export default function UserDashboard({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const [user,    setUser]    = useState<User | null>(null);
  const [posts,   setPosts]   = useState<Post[]>([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error,   setError]   = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then(r => { if (!r.ok) throw new Error('Failed'); return r.json(); })
      .then(setUser)
      .catch(() => setError('Could not load user'));
  }, [userId]); // ✅ userId in deps

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!user) return;
    setLoading(true);
    fetch(`/api/posts?author=${user.id}`)
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(data => { setPosts(data); setLoading(false); })
      .catch(() => setLoading(false)); // ✅ error handling
  }, [user]);

  function handleDeletePost(id: string) {
    setPosts(prev => prev.filter(post => post.id !== id)); // ✅ !== not ===
    fetch(`/api/posts/${id}`, { method: 'DELETE' });
  }

  const totalLikes = posts.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.likes, 0);

  if (error) return <p role="alert">{error}</p>;
  if (!user) return <p>Loading user...</p>; // ✅ null guard

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Welcome {user.name}</h1>
      <p>Total likes: {totalLikes}</p>
      {loading && <p>Loading posts...</p>}
      <ul>
        {posts.map(post => (
          <li key={post.id}> {/* ✅ key prop */}
            <span>{post.title}</span>
            <button onClick={() => handleDeletePost(post.id)}> {/* ✅ onClick */}
              Delete
            </button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}
```
"""

MESSAGE user "Why does my useEffect run infinitely?"
MESSAGE assistant """Infinite useEffect loops always come from one of these causes:

**Cause 1 — Object or array created inline in deps**
```tsx
// ❌ New object created every render → new reference → effect re-runs → re-render → repeat
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData(options);
}, [{ page: 1, limit: 10 }]); // new object every render

// ✅ Fix: use primitive values as deps
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData({ page, limit });
}, [page, limit]); // primitives compare by value

// ✅ Or memoize the object
const options = useMemo(() => ({ page: 1, limit: 10 }), []);
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData(options);
}, [options]);
```

**Cause 2 — Function created inline in deps**
```tsx
// ❌ New function reference every render
useEffect(() => {
  onMount();
}, [onMount]); // onMount is () => {} inline in parent = new ref every render

// ✅ Wrap in useCallback in the parent
const onMount = useCallback(() => {
  console.log('mounted');
}, []);

// ✅ Or if it's your own function, define it inside the effect
useEffect(() => {
  function fetchData() { /* ... */ }
  fetchData();
}, [userId]); // function defined inside has no identity problem
```

**Cause 3 — Setting state that's also a dependency**
```tsx
// ❌ data changes → effect runs → sets data → data changes → ...
useEffect(() => {
  setData(transform(data)); // data is both dep and set inside
}, [data]);

// ✅ Use functional update (doesn't need data as dep)
useEffect(() => {
  setData(prev => transform(prev));
}, []); // no dependency on data
```

**Cause 4 — Missing cleanup causing overlapping effects**
```tsx
// ❌ No cleanup — effects pile up
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount(c => c + 1), 1000);
  // no cleanup — interval keeps running after re-render
}, []);

// ✅ Always clean up subscriptions, intervals, timeouts
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount(c => c + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id); // cleanup
}, []);
```

**Debugging steps:**
1. Add `console.log('effect ran', deps)` inside the effect
2. Log each dep individually to see which one keeps changing
3. For objects/arrays, check if they're created inline
4. Use React DevTools Profiler to see what's triggering re-renders
"""

